>Again Hack's roots in (A)D&D show up. Hit Dice are used to determine how many
>hit points a creature has, and therefore a reliable guide to the monster's
>toughness.

More specifically, the Hit Dice is the number of 8-sided dice to be
rolled to determine the monster's hit points.  So if a dungeon master
was creating a 3HD monster for the players to deal with, he'd roll
3d8, or 3-24 hit points.  Usually there was a modified, i.e. HD: 3+10
means rolld 3d8 and add 10, for 13-34 hit points.

Player characters in AD&D also have hit dice, although the meaning is
slightly different.  It refers to the type of die you roll to
determine your new hit points at each experience level.  Barbarians
got a d12, while Magic Users got a d4.  Modifiers come from
constitution, which is another thing NetHack has borrowed.

